# {{ profile_name }}

An OSPREY build profile, materialized from the bundled `{{ preset_name }}`
preset. Everything the preset configures is written out here explicitly —
nothing is inherited at build time, and nothing is hidden inside the OSPREY
package. This directory is yours.

## The pipeline

```
PRESET  ──materialize──>  PROFILE DIR  ──build──>  PROJECT  ──deploy──>  RUNNING
(bundled)                 (this dir)               (derived)             (containers)
```

You are at step two. The preset was a starting point; this directory is now
the source of truth. The project you build from it is a derived artifact —
regenerable, and safe to delete and rebuild at any time.

## What you own, and what OSPREY owns

**Yours — edit freely, keep in version control:**

```
{{ profile_dirname }}/
  profile.yml     the full configuration
  .env.example    every variable the agent reads — documented, no values
  .env            your values{% if has_seeded_env %} (seeded from your shell){% else %} — create it from the example{% endif %}; never committed
  .gitignore      keeps .env and friends out of version control
  data/           facility content: channel databases, knowledge, lattice, scenarios
{% if has_triggers %}  triggers.yml    the events the agent runs on, for the dispatch block above
{% endif %}{% if persona_profiles %}  personas/       one delta per web-terminal persona, merged over profile.yml
{% endif %}{% if context_users %}  web-terminal-context/   one seeded directory per operator on the roster
{% for user in context_users %}    {{ user }}/ — an extra.md here is appended to {{ user }}'s context only
{% endfor %}{% endif %}  README.md       this file
```

Your own artifacts go in convention directories beside those — see
"Adding your own artifacts" below. Create the ones you need; a directory you
never create is simply one this profile contributes nothing to.

**OSPREY's — regenerated on every build, never hand-edited in the project:**
the `.claude/` scaffolding, `.mcp.json` wiring, the app templates, and the
container service definitions. (Hook *scripts* are the exception — they have a
`hooks/` directory of their own, described below. Their generated
configuration, `hook_config.json`, is not: it is derived from your
`mcp_servers:` and write-tool settings.)

## Building a project

```
osprey build <PROJECT_NAME> {{ profile_filename }}
```

Make durable changes here — in `profile.yml`, `data/`, or a convention
directory — then rebuild. Changes made directly inside a built project are lost
on the next build.

## Secrets

API keys and service credentials belong to this directory, not to the projects
built from it. `osprey build` derives a project's `.env` from the one here, so
a value you set once survives every rebuild — and a project you delete takes no
secret with it.

- `.env.example` lists every variable the agent reads, with no values. It is
  safe to commit, and it is the file to read when you want to know what can be
  set.
{% if has_seeded_env %}- `.env` holds the values. It was created for you from the provider keys your
  shell already exported, with `0600` permissions. Add anything else you need.
{% else %}- `.env` holds the values. Nothing was seeded — your shell exported no provider
  key — so create it yourself:

  ```
  cp {{ profile_dirname }}/.env.example {{ profile_dirname }}/.env
  ```
{% endif %}
`osprey deploy up` mints service credentials (database passwords, service
tokens) and writes them back here, so the stack keeps working across redeploys.
A value already on file is never overwritten.

The generated `.gitignore` keeps every `.env` variant out of git and lets
`.env.example` through. Keep it that way.

